#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2428fd3-c0b5-51a3-8140-ab5648378d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329263} {:answer/id #uuid "61090dbc-9ec4-5a3c-8b6c-8decc70cf6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329264} {:answer/id #uuid "6a259432-1b1e-5ebb-b9ae-c48fa783330e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329265} {:answer/id #uuid "32af292a-783e-5ab6-8c9b-03fbd575cebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329266} {:answer/id #uuid "88fb7b17-1892-534e-983a-5947c47d555a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a259432-1b1e-5ebb-b9ae-c48fa783330e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329265} :answer-field/id #uuid "acb9739b-df49-5f18-bb5b-0417ac37adcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bbe7f28-efd3-56e9-a3e7-84fe99064a40" :question/math-academy-id "q-109021" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}={x}^{3}{y}^{3/4}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3/4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{{y}^{1/4}}{x}={x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-3/4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,z={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4x}\\,z=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11354af7-b2c7-520f-95c1-717104e7bedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329270} {:answer/id #uuid "31df7db3-f90e-57fa-9036-59679e679851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329271} {:answer/id #uuid "0623ed2a-bdd6-5cae-b2c5-e807cdf30235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329272} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd19702-9c54-5140-ba6b-1087862af8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329273} {:answer/id #uuid "8638cc8e-5c91-5ab9-ba33-82dd1153cc34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0623ed2a-bdd6-5cae-b2c5-e807cdf30235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329272} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3de7a7-0d97-5379-80f9-4e237ee41ece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "023ae6da-320c-5d9f-893c-3cafd754c018" :question/math-academy-id "q-109024" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-xy=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{y}}{{x}^{5}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-x,\\,q(x)={x}^{-5},\\,n=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/3}\\frac{dy}{dx}-x{y}^{2/3}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{-1/3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-x\\,z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2x}{3}\\,z=\\frac{2}{3{x}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-\\frac{2x}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e26eb2a3-f820-5d12-b6dd-71854a0f9967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C-{e}^{5{x}^{3}}}{{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329277} {:answer/id #uuid "2b08c91a-aca9-5016-8503-b32cc4bb1e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{C{e}^{3{x}^{2}}-1}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329278} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5c237e-1202-5915-8012-04fdd3a5e0e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{C-{e}^{3{x}^{2}}}{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329279} {:answer/id #uuid "cb18262f-5203-5e13-acf6-8c9af8fb1ef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{x}-1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329280} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d54925-04a5-52ae-8425-88472a7940b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4d54925-04a5-52ae-8425-88472a7940b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329281} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e4b3a8-2ec3-5c49-a6cd-2f7311b25459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90997a63-8b0a-5ca6-9670-b13bb0d8de17" :question/math-academy-id "q-109027" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}y={x}^{2}{y}^{-4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-3{x}^{2},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=-4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}\\frac{dy}{dx}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{5}={x}^{2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5{y}^{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z=5{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-15{x}^{2},\\,Q(x)=5{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-15{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z) & ={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot (5{x}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} & =5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}) & =5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{5}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{5}=\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}\\,⇒\\,y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c1b73a-ddb0-5185-a9d2-4295e6b3ab74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329284} {:answer/id #uuid "b312561b-17a7-5f1b-9e25-632d8ccc99c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{C{e}^{x}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329285} {:answer/id #uuid "55e0d93a-8572-52e1-a086-959899b473e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2x}{C+{e}^{2x}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329286} {:answer/id #uuid "c26b94f6-7d3f-5f6e-a2cd-3fe2d88b4bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x+1}{C-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329287} {:answer/id #uuid "ced8eb65-de8c-580d-9481-5a07bfd6fd04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{C{e}^{x}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08c1b73a-ddb0-5185-a9d2-4295e6b3ab74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329284} :answer-field/id #uuid "581e3af7-db14-5b36-b226-a78708939c61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21b39c4d-cfb3-59e7-bfe5-8b7a15dc1142" :question/math-academy-id "q-109028" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-x{y}^{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=1,\\,q(x)=-x,\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{-2}=-x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z=2x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-2,\\,Q(x)=2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z) & ={e}^{-2x}\\cdot (2x) \\\\ {e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z{e}^{-2x} & =2x{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x}) & =2x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-2x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}(2x+1)+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-2}=\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04841c1a-60c3-57db-91b5-dcbeab6a0fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-5{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329291} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7699e8-a6fa-5ae7-bd4a-9cb54c850ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{C+{x}^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329292} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b16c4a-1802-5e22-9ce7-ae7b641fb6c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329293} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd95274-821b-5b36-a4b3-a6b7d28a48db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{2x}{C-{x}^{3/2}})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329294} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe0dfa2-f184-50ee-ae9d-bd46cd9ee6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{C+{x}^{5/2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9b16c4a-1802-5e22-9ce7-ae7b641fb6c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329293} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f367fc4-b717-5b6e-81f2-788d821286e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "692fec97-1d1e-5270-a23f-3b8552e51e1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109154" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{-1}=\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\sqrt{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-x\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
